      "latency_ms": 12606.5,
      "prompt_tokens": 28,
      "completion_tokens": 1127,
      "output": "**Water** is a transparent, tasteless, odorless, and nearly colorless chemical substance that is the main constituent of Earth's hydrosphere and the fluids of all known living organisms. It is essential for all known forms of life.\n\nHere is a breakdown of what water is from chemical, physical, biological, and planetary perspectives:\n\n---\n\n### 1. Chemical Identity\n*   **Formula:** **H₂O** (two hydrogen atoms covalently bonded to one oxygen atom).\n*   **Molecular Structure:** The molecule is **bent (V-shaped)** at an angle of ~104.5°. Oxygen is highly electronegative, pulling electrons away from the hydrogen atoms. This creates a **polar molecule** with a partial negative charge near the oxygen and partial positive charges near the hydrogens.\n*   **Hydrogen Bonding:** This polarity causes water molecules to stick to one another via **hydrogen bonds** (attraction between the H of one molecule and the O of another). This relatively weak but abundant bonding is the secret behind almost all of water’s unique properties.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Physical States (Phases)\nWater is one of the few substances that exists naturally on Earth in all three states:\n*   **Solid (Ice):** Forms at **0°C (32°F)** at standard pressure. Uniquely, ice is **less dense than liquid water** (it expands ~9% upon freezing), causing it to float. This insulates bodies of water, allowing life to survive underneath.\n*   **Liquid (Water):** Exists between 0°C and 100°C. It has high **surface tension**, **capillary action**, and **viscosity** due to hydrogen bonding.\n*   **Gas (Water Vapor/Steam):** Boils at **100°C (212°F)** at standard pressure. It is a potent **greenhouse gas** and drives global weather patterns.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Key Anomalous Properties (Why Water is \"Weird\")\nWater behaves differently than almost any other liquid due to hydrogen bonding:\n*   **Universal Solvent:** Its polarity allows it to dissolve more substances than any other liquid (salts, sugars, acids, bases, gases), enabling chemical transport in biology and geology.\n*   **High Specific Heat Capacity:** It takes a lot of energy to heat water up. This **buffers temperature changes** on Earth (coastal climates are milder) and stabilizes internal body temperatures in organisms.\n*   **High Heat of Vaporization:** Evaporating water consumes massive energy. This makes **sweating/panting** highly effective cooling mechanisms for animals.\n*   **Density Maximum at 4°C:** Water is densest at 4°C, not at its freezing point. This causes **lake turnover** in seasons, oxygenating deep water.\n*   **Cohesion & Adhesion:** Water sticks to itself (cohesion) and other surfaces (adhesion), allowing **capillary action** (water moving up plant roots/trunks against gravity).\n\n---\n\n### 4. Biological Role\n*   **Medium of Life:** All known biochemical reactions occur in aqueous solution.\n*   **Transport:** Blood (mostly water) transports nutrients, oxygen, and waste.\n*   **Structure:** Water pressure (turgor pressure) gives plant cells rigidity; hydrostatic skeletons in worms/jellyfish rely on water.\n*   **Reactant:** Essential for **hydrolysis** (breaking down polymers like proteins/DNA) and **photosynthesis** (6CO₂ + 6H₂O → C₆H₁₂O₆ + 6O₂).\n\n---\n\n### 5. Planetary Distribution (The Hydrosphere)\n*   **~71% of Earth's surface** is covered by water.\n*   **97.5% is Saline** (oceans).\n*   **2.5% is Freshwater**, but most is locked in:\n    *   Ice caps/glaciers (~68.7% of freshwater).\n    *   Groundwater (~30.1%).\n    *   **< 1% of freshwater** (0.007% of total water) is easily accessible in lakes, rivers, and soil moisture for human use.\n\n---\n\n### 6. Human Context\n*   **Consumption:** Humans can survive only ~3 days without water. The body is ~60% water (varies by age/fat).\n*   **Agriculture:** ~70% of global freshwater withdrawals go to irrigation.\n*   **Industry/Energy:** Used for cooling (thermal power plants), manufacturing, and hydroelectric power.\n*   **Sanitation:** Critical for hygiene and disease prevention.\n\n---\n\n### Summary\nWater is **not just a resource; it is the matrix of life.** Its simple molecular structure (H₂O) creates complex emergent properties—floating ice, high heat capacity, universal solvency—that make Earth habitable and biology possible."
